description.narrative:
In recent years, rapid climate change has posed a threat to habitats and natural resources, impacting future economic development. In this context, sustainable development has become a key concern not only for governments around the world but also for global organizations and businesses.

As the regulatory authority in the banking sector, the State Bank of Vietnam (SBV) has issued several legal documents related to promoting sustainable development, including:

Circular No. 17/2022/TT-NHNN dated December 23, 2023, guidingthe management of environmental risks in credit activities of credit institutions and foreign bank branches in Vietnam;
Decision No. 1604/QĐ-NHNN dated August 7, 2018, approving the Green Banking Development Project in Vietnam;
Decision No. 1663/QĐ-NHNN dated August 6, 2024, amending and supplementing certain articles of Decision No. 1604/QĐ-NHNN on the approval of the Green Banking Development Project in Vietnam.
The overarching goal is to raise awareness and social responsibility within the banking system regarding environmental protection and climate change mitigation, gradually greening banking operations, directing credit flows toward environmentally friendly projects, and promoting green production, services, consumption, clean energy, and renewable energy—thereby contributing positively to green growth and sustainable development.

In addition to the general goal, the SBV has set out three specific objectives:

Gradually improve access to green capital for credit institutions and increase the proportion of credit for projects listedin the national green classification;
Promote the application of technology alongside building environmentally friendly habits for customers within banking activities; strongly develop electronic transaction channels, new services, and payment methods based on modern technology;
Strive by 2025 to have more credit institutions managing environmental and social risks in credit activities, aligning with international standards, including:
100% of credit institutions and foreign bank branches establishing internal regulationson environmental risk management in credit activities;
100% of credit institutions and foreign bank branches assessing environmental risks in credit activities; applying environmental standards to projects receiving bank credit; integrating environmental risk assessment as part of credit risk evaluation;
At least 10–12 banks having dedicated units or departments for environmental risk management.
The implementation of this project will contribute to achieving the set goals and plans, enhancing awareness, knowledge, and capacity in sustainable finance. It will encourage banks to develop and offer green and sustainable financial products and services intheir business operations, thereby creating positive impacts on the community and the environment. Through research, evaluation, and training activities, the project will help banks better understand the benefits and business models of green finance, providing them withthe necessary tools and skills to implement sustainable financial products and services. It will also support banks in developing and implementing internal policies and procedures to promote sustainable finance. Promoting sustainable finance in Vietnamesecommercial banks will also help boost green capital and green transformation among enterprises, contributing to the Vietnamese Government’s strategy for green growth and sustainable development.
